You didn't include the actual tier data, so I've sketched a typical three-tier SaaS lineup (Basic / Pro / Team) with representative prices and specs — swap in your real numbers and the reasoning holds.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp1, met1, tbl1])
cmp1 = Comparison("Tier comparison", [tierA, tierB, tierC])
tierA = {name:"Basic", attributes:["$12 / month","1 vCPU","1 GB RAM","10 GB storage","Email support"]}
tierB = {name:"Pro", attributes:["$29 / month","2 vCPU","4 GB RAM","50 GB storage","Priority support"]}
tierC = {name:"Team", attributes:["$79 / month","4 vCPU","8 GB RAM","200 GB storage","SSO + audit log"]}
met1 = Metric("Cost per GB RAM", "$7.25", "Pro tier — lowest of the three", "positive")
tbl1 = Table("Unit economics", ["Tier","Price / mo","RAM","Cost per GB RAM","Cost per vCPU"], [["Basic","$12","1 GB","$12.00","$12.00"],["Pro","$29","4 GB","$7.25","$14.50"],["Team","$79","8 GB","$9.88","$19.75"]])
:::

**Pro is the best value** — it's the only tier where you pay *less* per unit of capacity than the tier below it ($7.25 per GB of RAM versus $12.00 on Basic), while Team charges a premium for SSO and audit logging rather than for raw capacity.

Two caveats: Basic only makes sense if you genuinely sit at ~1 GB of RAM, and Team becomes the right call the moment you need SSO for compliance — that's a feature purchase, not a value one. If you share the real tiers, I'll redo the per-unit math against them.

